Obtain energy entropy and response from a normalized thermal model (@physics-statistical-canonical: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Distinguish-concentration-correlations-and-uncertainty-in-an-observation.lingua#r_00BB3DEB755830AE5E94CAF4BA") { r_008071436CDCAF56B4074E84AD
For a fixed finite set of microstates with temperature-independent energies E_i, define Z(β)=Σ_i exp(−βE_i) and π_i=exp(−βE_i)/Z. Z is positive, and π is normalized. A common shift E_i→E_i+c multiplies Z by exp(−βc), leaves π unchanged and shifts the mean energy by c. Changing the energy spacing or degeneracy changes the probabilities. Degeneracy belongs in the sum when grouping energies.

Finite differentiation gives U=Σπ_i E_i=−∂ln Z/∂β. A second derivative yields ∂²ln Z/∂β²=Σπ_i E_i²−U²=Var(E), hence ∂U/∂β=−Var(E). At positive T, β=1/(k_B T) and C=(∂U/∂T) with the same fixed levels satisfies C=Var(E)/(k_B T²). The equality requires the canonical model and its fixed external parameters; an isolated total energy has zero variance and does not obey this inference merely by using the same temperature symbol. Countable sums need the convergence/operation-exchange justification developed in mathematics.

Substitution into the statistical entropy gives S=k_B(ln Z+βU). Differentiating along this fixed-level equilibrium family gives dS=k_Bβ dU=dU/T. With no changing levels or other work coordinate, this is consistent with reversible heat dQ_rev=dU and the existing thermodynamic entropy relation. It is a restricted correspondence, not a derivation of every thermodynamic process. Define Helmholtz energy F=U−TS=−k_B T ln Z, called A in [Choose chemical constraints before using a free energy sign](../../chemistry/core/Choose-chemical-constraints-before-using-a-free-energy-sign.lingua#r_0024B2FCA41783BA78949E37E5); the symbol change does not change the quantity or its constraints.

A finite proof explains the equilibrium minimum. For any distribution p on these states, set D(p||π)=Σ_(p_i>0)p_i ln(p_i/π_i). The inequality ln x≤x−1 follows by differentiating x−1−ln x, whose minimum is zero at x=1. Applying it to π_i/p_i and summing gives D≥Σ_(p_i>0)(p_i−π_i)≥0. Equality requires p=π. Therefore

[Σp_i E_i−T S[p]]−F=k_B T D(p||π)≥0.

This is a variational statement at fixed T and state energies. It does not prove that arbitrary dynamics decrease that expression. If p has the same mean energy as π, the same identity proves S[p]≤S[π]. The microscopic statistical model and the thermodynamic work-bound account are connected but retain their separate premises.

For a sites with independent energies 0 and ε, put r=exp(−βε). Then Z=(1+r)^a, excitation probability p=r/(1+r), U=aεp and Var(E)=aε²p(1−p). The grouping by excitation count has P(J=j)=C(a,j)r^j/(1+r)^a. With r=3/5 and a=3 the weights are (125,225,135,27)/512, U=9ε/8 and Var(E)=45ε²/64. This agrees in mean with the finite isolated fixture but has a larger variance. Finite weights with r=0 or r=1 are useful limiting tables; r>1 is also mathematically normalizable here, although it is outside the stated positive-temperature interpretation for ε>0.

**Bounded published reconstruction:** [Statistical Physics — David Tong](../../engineering/materials-engineering/sources/Statistical-Physics-Tong.lingua#r_7FMG47HD1MT706NT3CABRTFNS2), section 1.3.2, printed page 21, gives a two-state canonical example. With r=exp(−βε), tanh(βε/2)=(1−r)/(1+r), so its mean-energy expression becomes aεr/(1+r). Our checker compares that algebraic form with direct configuration enumeration; it does not reproduce the source's experimental heat-capacity figure.

**Work:** derive every differentiation identity and identify the fixed quantities. Prove energy-reference invariance and the relative-entropy inequality, including zero entries of p. Change the excited-state degeneracy to two and recompute Z and the mean. Read the remaining energy/entropy/free-energy sections of chapter 1 progressively; full assigned reading and problem sheets remain learner work.

